- How would you return the exact cycle path rather than just edge pairs?
- What are the time and space complexities for dense vs sparse graphs?

## Concept Overview
An LRU (Least Recently Used) cache efficiently manages memory by evicting the least accessed items when capacity is reached. This implementation leverages a HashMap for fast key lookups and a DoublyLinkedList for maintaining access order.

## Implementation Details
- **HashMap**: Maps keys to Node references, enabling O(1) lookup operations
- **DoublyLinkedList**: Maintains access order with head representing most recently used items and tail representing least recently used items
- **Thread Safety**: ReentrantReadWriteLock provides optimal concurrency - multiple threads can read simultaneously while writes are mutually exclusive
- **Eviction Policy**: When capacity is exceeded, the tail node (least recently used) is removed
- **Operations**: Both get() and put() operations maintain O(1) time complexity

## Code Example
```java
public class LRUCache<K, V> {
    private final Map<K, Node<K, V>> cache;
    private final DoublyLinkedList<K, V> order;
    private final ReentrantReadWriteLock lock;
    private final int capacity;

    public LRUCache(int capacity) {
        this.capacity = capacity;
        this.cache = new HashMap<>();
        this.order = new DoublyLinkedList<>();
        this.lock = new ReentrantReadWriteLock();
    }

    public V get(K key) {
        lock.readLock().lock();
        try {
            Node<K, V> node = cache.get(key);
            if (node != null) {
                // Upgrade to write lock for reordering
                lock.readLock().unlock();
                lock.writeLock().lock();
                order.moveToHead(node);
                return node.value;
            }
            return null;
        } finally {
            if (lock.isWriteLocked()) {
                lock.writeLock().unlock();
            } else {
                lock.readLock().unlock();
            }
        }
    }

    public void put(K key, V value) {
        lock.writeLock().lock();
        try {
            Node<K, V> existing = cache.get(key);
            if (existing != null) {
                existing.value = value;
                order.moveToHead(existing);
                return;
            }

            Node<K, V> newNode = new Node<>(key, value);
            cache.put(key, newNode);
            order.addToHead(newNode);

            if (cache.size() > capacity) {
                Node<K, V> tail = order.removeTail();
                cache.remove(tail.key);
            }
        } finally {
            lock.writeLock().unlock();
        }
    }
}```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to design augmented self-balancing BSTs for dynamic range queries—a common requirement in analytics and data processing applications.

## Key Concepts

- **AVL balancing**: Maintaining height balance factor (|left.height - right.height| ≤ 1) through rotations
- **Augmented BST data**: Storing additional aggregate information (cnt, sum) at each node
- **Range query on BST**: Using the BST property to efficiently prune irrelevant subtrees
- **Correctness and complexity analysis**: Ensuring O(log n) operations and verifying invariants

## Code Example

```javascript
class Node {
  constructor(key) {
    this.key = key;
    this.cnt = 1;
    this.height = 1;
    this.left = null;
    this.right = null;
    this.sum = key;
  }
}

function getHeight(node) { return node ? node.height : 0; }
function getSum(node) { return node ? node.sum : 0; }
function update(node) {
  node.height = 1 + Math.max(getHeight(node.left), getHeight(node.right));
  node.sum = getSum(node.left) + node.key * node.cnt + getSum(node.right);
}

function rightRotate(y) {
  const x = y.left;
  y.left = x.right;
  x.right = y;
  update(y);
  update(x);
  return x;
}

function rangeSum(node, a, b) {
  if (!node) return 0;
  if (node.key < a) return rangeSum(node.right, a, b);
  if (node.key > b) return rangeSum(node.left, a, b);
  return getSum(node.left) + node.key * node.cnt + rangeSum(node.right, a, b);
}
```

